{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 760 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "74e6945d889ea30865b091b536e3c6a2ca45658b26cd7097e6efe315170afbf3" :edb/db-before-t 759 :edb/db-after-t 760 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534072 50 #inst "2026-10-06T02:41:12.790Z" 13194139534072 true] [17592186096627 1020 #edb/ref 17592186276656 13194139534072 true] [17592186096627 1020 #edb/ref 17592186276663 13194139534072 true] [17592186096627 1020 #edb/ref 17592186276670 13194139534072 true] [17592186096629 1020 #edb/ref 17592186276653 13194139534072 true] [17592186097039 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534072 true] [17592186097039 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/assets/cac89f66c8c1e5e0ac6a9857cb7db48d3ca6edc2a83fb2ba3a63b0809cfe6ab0.png)\n\nWhat is the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" 13194139534072 true] [17592186097039 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1132/Source/Images/q-14248.png)\n\nWhat is the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" 13194139534072 false] [17592186097039 1145 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(5,-4)$ and passes through the point $(2,5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(5,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}=4p(y+4).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(2,5),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-5)^{2} & =4p(5+4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5)^{2} & =y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-5)^{2} & =y+4 \\\\ 25 & =y+4 \\\\ y & =25-4 \\\\ y & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $21.$" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276653 1017 #edb/ref 17592186276654 13194139534072 true] [17592186276653 1089 "q-251796" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276653 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534072 true] [17592186276653 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/assets/cec5eacc688d3c3a619aeb6ede2b1c9f9290b8045699259d4c1d933ecefd1835.png)\n\nThe value of the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above  is{{field-1}}." 13194139534072 true] [17592186276653 1113 #uuid "feea1d8a-6328-5eb2-9e54-6502ed361d20" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276653 1145 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(4,-6)$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(4,-6)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}=4p(y+6).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,3),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2-4)^{2} & =4p(3+6) \\\\ 36 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4(y+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2} & =4(y+6) \\\\ 16 & =4(y+6) \\\\ 4 & =y+6 \\\\ y & =4-6 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $-2.$" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276654 1000 #edb/ref 17592186276655 13194139534072 true] [17592186276654 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534072 true] [17592186276654 1041 "field-1" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276654 1104 #edb/ref 17592186276655 13194139534072 true] [17592186276654 1110 #uuid "d57ef979-b2c9-58f3-af3d-2c9605e2a13f" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276655 1034 "-2" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276655 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534072 true] [17592186276655 1111 #uuid "67eeaded-d494-5d1a-960d-f93269f0bed1" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276656 1017 #edb/ref 17592186276657 13194139534072 true] [17592186276656 1089 "q-73831" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276656 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534072 true] [17592186276656 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/assets/0a9d728a5927f380bbc88445c6a409974d94050040f23000cce50f70de436917.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276656 1113 #uuid "0efcadc6-2824-5745-889c-95d91d10c622" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276656 1145 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-3,-1)$ and passes through the point $(-6,8).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-3,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =4p(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-6,8),$ we substitute $x=-6$ and $y=8$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6+3)^{2} & =4p(8+1) \\\\ 9 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =(0+1) \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $-4$ and $-2.$" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276657 1000 #edb/ref 17592186276658 13194139534072 true] [17592186276657 1000 #edb/ref 17592186276659 13194139534072 true] [17592186276657 1000 #edb/ref 17592186276660 13194139534072 true] [17592186276657 1000 #edb/ref 17592186276661 13194139534072 true] [17592186276657 1000 #edb/ref 17592186276662 13194139534072 true] [17592186276657 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534072 true] [17592186276657 1041 "selection" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276657 1104 #edb/ref 17592186276658 13194139534072 true] [17592186276657 1110 #uuid "3a7ad1a6-e720-513d-b510-c1eb2349eae8" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276658 1034 "$-4$ and $-2$" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276658 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534072 true] [17592186276658 1111 #uuid "6b67ce46-4a76-5e53-baf2-737f4bbdf1d5" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276659 1034 "$-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $-\\frac{5}{2}$" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276659 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534072 true] [17592186276659 1111 #uuid "83021b32-a7d8-52b3-a794-f05e6aecda9a" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276660 1034 "$-\\frac{13}{3}$ and $-\\frac{5}{3}$" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276660 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534072 true] [17592186276660 1111 #uuid "e45e5e77-787d-541a-b6e1-0d4af09eb422" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276661 1034 "$-\\sqrt{6}-1$ and $-\\sqrt{6}+1$" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276661 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534072 true] [17592186276661 1111 #uuid "056dcd5a-4083-5525-a6e6-63cdaa7d363b" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276662 1034 "$-5$ and $-1$" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276662 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534072 true] [17592186276662 1111 #uuid "cb012d8a-9de1-5b74-911d-afed583d3f2a" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276663 1017 #edb/ref 17592186276664 13194139534072 true] [17592186276663 1089 "q-73833" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276663 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534072 true] [17592186276663 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/assets/9a7f256ae77447f4b426612a3e136ccb60ebd172a6eb472c9fa9948e324b3085.png)\n\nWhat is the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276663 1113 #uuid "5f07bf12-0aed-5e39-ac42-865205552e47" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276663 1145 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-8,-3)$ and passes through the point $(-2,-6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-8,-3)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+3)^{2}=4p(x+8).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,-6),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=-6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6+3)^{2} & =4p(-2+8) \\\\ 9 & =4p(6) \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2}(x+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+3)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2}(x+8) \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x+8) \\\\ \\frac{18}{3} & =x+8 \\\\ x & =6-8 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $-2.$" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276664 1000 #edb/ref 17592186276665 13194139534072 true] [17592186276664 1000 #edb/ref 17592186276666 13194139534072 true] [17592186276664 1000 #edb/ref 17592186276667 13194139534072 true] [17592186276664 1000 #edb/ref 17592186276668 13194139534072 true] [17592186276664 1000 #edb/ref 17592186276669 13194139534072 true] [17592186276664 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534072 true] [17592186276664 1041 "selection" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276664 1104 #edb/ref 17592186276669 13194139534072 true] [17592186276664 1110 #uuid "fb3fc613-d832-5aed-aabc-383ce99aedc0" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276665 1034 "-2\\sqrt{2}" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276665 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534072 true] [17592186276665 1111 #uuid "5e34980b-eda0-5d41-a647-b83acca98df5" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276666 1034 "-4" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276666 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534072 true] [17592186276666 1111 #uuid "8bb0407b-d482-565f-9d13-63529eadf4af" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276667 1034 "-3" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276667 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534072 true] [17592186276667 1111 #uuid "81cdc982-59e6-561e-bf1e-76eff41e1f73" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276668 1034 "-\\sqrt{2}" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276668 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534072 true] [17592186276668 1111 #uuid "444c5e7d-d63d-5c26-9878-c76dd8db52c3" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276669 1034 "-2" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276669 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534072 true] [17592186276669 1111 #uuid "ffa01371-cd88-5d1b-90d2-b9ee19dda065" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276670 1017 #edb/ref 17592186276671 13194139534072 true] [17592186276670 1089 "q-79252" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276670 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534072 true] [17592186276670 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/assets/aa95f5a4629b2f97943db55125f91d76de962534be820fe70112132e5fddaaec.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276670 1113 #uuid "248b559b-2f7e-5113-8ca0-3e8f78ffa304" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276670 1145 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-1,-4)$ and passes through the point $(2,5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-1,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(2,5),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2+1)^{2} & =4p(5+4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =0+4 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =1,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $1$ and $-3.$" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276671 1000 #edb/ref 17592186276672 13194139534072 true] [17592186276671 1000 #edb/ref 17592186276673 13194139534072 true] [17592186276671 1000 #edb/ref 17592186276674 13194139534072 true] [17592186276671 1000 #edb/ref 17592186276675 13194139534072 true] [17592186276671 1000 #edb/ref 17592186276676 13194139534072 true] [17592186276671 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534072 true] [17592186276671 1041 "selection" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276671 1104 #edb/ref 17592186276672 13194139534072 true] [17592186276671 1110 #uuid "1bfe0323-745f-532d-bd98-762c74aab9ce" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276672 1034 "$1$ and $-3$" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276672 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534072 true] [17592186276672 1111 #uuid "bb823ae9-bd88-55b8-a6f4-d9765841e219" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276673 1034 "$\\frac{3}{2}$ and $-\\frac{5}{2}$" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276673 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534072 true] [17592186276673 1111 #uuid "8b5a355f-f347-5ffa-ba32-286317332e5a" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276674 1034 "$\\frac{5}{2}$ and $-\\frac{7}{2}$" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276674 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534072 true] [17592186276674 1111 #uuid "2a8dd277-0a70-5f39-bfad-c90f2f0b3e5a" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276675 1034 "$3$ and $-5$" 13194139534072 true] [17592186276675 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534072 true] [17592186276675 1111 #uuid "a9e13235-fd